WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 7.1: Hyper-V and Virtual Switches. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 7.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.850
1, Hyper-V and Virtual Switches.

3
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.300
This learning plan turns one server into
many.

4
00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:13.150
First you learn what virtualization is,
and the difference between type 1 and type

5
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.600
2 hypervisors.

6
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:19.250
You see how Hyper-V is built, with
partitions and the VMBus.

7
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.300
You check the host hardware and the
license before you install anything.

8
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:29.350
Then you install Hyper-V with Server
Manager and PowerShell, set the host

9
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:35.200
defaults, and build the three kinds of
virtual switches that every later lab in

10
00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:36.650
this plan uses.

11
00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:42.100
Picture three physical servers: one for
DNS, one for DHCP, one for files.

12
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.350
Each one sits mostly idle.

13
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.800
Virtualization lets one physical computer
run all three as virtual machines, or VMs.

14
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:54.850
Each VM acts like its own computer with
its own operating system.

15
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.300
Three terms.

16
00:00:56.550 --> 00:01:02.950
The host is the physical computer that
runs the VMs; in our pod that is HV01.

17
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.600
A guest is a VM and the operating system
inside it.

18
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:13.450
And the hypervisor is the software that
shares the hardware and keeps the VMs

19
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:14.900
apart.

20
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.150
Hypervisors come in two types.

21
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:23.800
A type 1 hypervisor runs directly on the
hardware, which is why it is called bare

22
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.250
metal.

23
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:30.300
Hyper-V and VMware ESXi are type 1, and
servers use this type.

24
00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:37.350
A type 2 hypervisor is an app that runs on
top of a normal operating system, like

25
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.400
VMware Workstation or VirtualBox on a
desktop.

26
00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:46.650
Type 1 is faster and more secure, because
nothing sits between it and the hardware.

27
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:52.500
Yet Hyper-V is type 1 even though you
install it as a Windows role.

28
00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.750
The next slide shows why.

29
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.400
With Hyper-V installed, the Windows
hypervisor loads first, on the hardware.

30
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:06.050
Then your Windows Server starts on top of
it in the root partition, also called the

31
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:07.500
parent partition.

32
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:13.350
It is the only partition with direct
access to the hardware and the real

33
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:14.800
drivers.

34
00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.250
It creates a child partition for each VM.

35
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.700
A child partition reaches devices only
through the VMBus, a fast channel between

36
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:25.950
partitions, or through the hypervisor.

37
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:32.200
In each VM, clients called VSCs send
device requests over the VMBus to VSPs in

38
00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:33.650
the root.

39
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.300
Before you install, check the hardware.

40
00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:41.750
You need a 64-bit processor with SLAT,
second level address translation, so the

41
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.000
processor maps VM memory itself.

42
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:49.050
You need VM Monitor Mode extensions, which
means Intel VT or AMD-V.

43
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:54.900
Virtualization must be turned on in the
BIOS or UEFI, often off by default.

44
00:02:55.150 --> 00:03:01.150
And hardware Data Execution Prevention
must be on: the XD bit on Intel, the NX

45
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:02.600
bit on AMD.

46
00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:06.050
Run systeminfo and look for four Yes
lines.

47
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.500
If a hypervisor is already running, you
see a message saying so instead.

48
00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:12.950
Remember Topic 1.

49
00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:14.400
2.

50
00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:19.850
The edition decides how many Windows
Server VMs a licensed host may run.

51
00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.100
Standard edition allows two, when all
physical cores are licensed.

52
00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:27.950
For two more, you license all the cores
again.

53
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.000
Datacenter, which our pod runs, allows
unlimited Windows Server VMs on the

54
00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:34.450
licensed host.

55
00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:41.100
Like a Server Plus technician, size the
host for itself plus every VM that runs at

56
00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:42.550
once.

57
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.400
Plan for failure: one host now carries
many servers.

58
00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:51.450
And guests that are not Windows Server
follow their own license terms.

59
00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:54.500
Installing Hyper-V starts like any other
role.

60
00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:58.750
In Server Manager, open Manage, then Add
Roles and Features.

61
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.600
On the Server Roles page, check Hyper-V
and select Add Features, so the management

62
00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:06.050
tools come along.

63
00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.100
Hyper-V adds three pages of its own.

64
00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:14.550
On Virtual Switches, select no adapter:
you create the switches later, with the

65
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.400
names the labs expect.

66
00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:22.250
On Migration and Default Stores, leave the
defaults; you change the paths in Hyper-V

67
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:23.700
Settings.

68
00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:28.350
On the Confirmation page, check Restart
the destination server automatically if

69
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:30.200
required, then select Install.

70
00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:33.250
Here is the same job in PowerShell.

71
00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:38.300
Get-WindowsFeature with the name Hyper-V
shows Available, so it is not installed

72
00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:39.750
yet.

73
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.600
Then run Install-WindowsFeature with the
name Hyper-V, dash IncludeManagementTools,

74
00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.050
and dash Restart.

75
00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:50.100
The server restarts by itself, possibly
twice, because the hypervisor can only

76
00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:51.550
load at boot.

77
00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.400
Sign in again and run Get-WindowsFeature
for Hyper-V star.

78
00:04:55.650 --> 00:05:00.850
Now the role, the Hyper-V module and the
GUI tools all show Installed.

79
00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:05.900
On Server Core, that switch adds only the
PowerShell module, nothing else.

80
00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:10.150
Open Hyper-V Manager from the Tools menu
in Server Manager.

81
00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.800
On the left you see Hyper-V Manager and
the host, HV01.

82
00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:21.050
The middle shows the Virtual Machines
list, which is empty on a new host, then

83
00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.500
Checkpoints, and a details pane at the
bottom.

84
00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:29.550
On the right, the Actions pane holds the
jobs for the host.

85
00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.800
New creates a virtual machine or a virtual
hard disk.

86
00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.050
Hyper-V Settings holds host-wide defaults.

87
00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:40.300
And Virtual Switch Manager is where you
build virtual networks.

88
00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:42.550
You use all three today.

89
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.200
Hyper-V Settings holds the host defaults.

90
00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:51.050
Under Virtual Hard Disks, set the folder
for disk files, C colon Hyper-V Disks.

91
00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.500
Under Virtual Machines, set C colon
Hyper-V VMs.

92
00:05:54.750 --> 00:06:00.750
Enhanced Session Mode gives you a Remote
Desktop style connection to a VM, and it

93
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.600
needs two check boxes.

94
00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:07.250
Under Server, Enhanced Session Mode
Policy, check Allow enhanced session mode.

95
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.500
Under User, Enhanced Session Mode, check
Use enhanced session mode.

96
00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:17.750
Set-VMHost sets the paths and the server
policy; the user setting is per user, in

97
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.200
Hyper-V Manager only.

98
00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:25.050
A virtual switch is a software network
switch inside the host, and every VM

99
00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:26.900
adapter plugs into one.

100
00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:28.750
There are three types.

101
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:34.600
An External switch binds to a physical
network adapter, so VMs reach the real

102
00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:37.650
network like any server on the LAN.

103
00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:39.100
Think bridged networking.

104
00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:44.950
An Internal switch connects the VMs and
the host, but not the physical network.

105
00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:48.400
Think host-only networking, great for a
test lab.

106
00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:51.450
A Private switch connects only the VMs.

107
00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:54.100
Even the host cannot reach them.

108
00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:58.750
In Virtual Switch Manager, select New
virtual network switch, choose External,

109
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.000
and select Create Virtual Switch.

110
00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:02.450
Name it External.

111
00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:05.500
Under Connection type, pick the physical
adapter.

112
00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:10.950
The list shows the adapter's description,
not its name, so match it with

113
00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:12.400
Get-NetAdapter first.

114
00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:17.850
Keep Allow management operating system to
share this network adapter checked, so the

115
00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:19.700
host keeps its network.

116
00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:23.550
Leave the VLAN ID box off in the pod.

117
00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:29.400
When you select OK, a warning says the
network may drop for a moment.

118
00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:30.850
Select Yes.

119
00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.100
What happens behind the scenes?

120
00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:37.750
The physical adapter, Ethernet, becomes
only an uplink for the switch.

121
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.200
Because sharing is allowed, Windows adds a
virtual adapter for the host, named

122
00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:44.650
vEthernet External,.

123
00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:47.700
The host's IP settings move to it.

124
00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:49.550
So HV01's address, 192.

125
00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:51.000
168.

126
00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:52.450
50.

127
00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:55.100
30, now sits on vEthernet External.

128
00:07:55.350 --> 00:08:00.950
An Internal switch also gives the host a
vEthernet adapter; a Private switch gives

129
00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:02.400
none.

130
00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:07.850
The VLAN ID box would tag the host's
traffic with a VLAN number.

131
00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:13.300
Use it only when the physical switch port
is set up for it.

132
00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:15.150
Now the PowerShell way.

133
00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:19.400
New-VMSwitch with dash NetAdapterName
creates an external switch; naming an

134
00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:22.050
adapter is what makes it external.

135
00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:26.300
Add dash AllowManagementOS true to share
it with the host.

136
00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:30.550
For the other two, use dash SwitchType
Internal or Private.

137
00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:33.600
Get-VMSwitch lists all three with their
types.

138
00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:38.250
Get-NetAdapter now shows two new host
adapters, vEthernet External and vEthernet

139
00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:39.700
Internal.

140
00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:43.150
Last, New-NetIPAddress gives vEthernet
Internal the address 10.

141
00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:44.600
10.

142
00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:46.050
10.

143
00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:49.500
1 with prefix length 24 and no gateway.

144
00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:53.750
VMs on the internal network use it in Lab
7.

145
00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:55.200
2.

146
00:08:55.450 --> 00:09:01.050
Each VM gets its own virtual network
adapter, plugged into one switch, and its

147
00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:04.900
settings live in the VM's settings under
Network Adapter.

148
00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:08.350
You can change the switch at any time.

149
00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:14.600
Bandwidth management sets a minimum and a
maximum, so one VM cannot hog the physical

150
00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.050
adapter.

151
00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:21.900
MAC address spoofing lets a VM send from
other MAC addresses, which nested VMs

152
00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:23.350
need.

153
00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:29.600
DHCP guard and router guard drop DHCP or
router messages from VMs that should not

154
00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:31.050
send them.

155
00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:33.700
You use them in Labs 7.

156
00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:35.150
2 and 7.

157
00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:36.600
3.

158
00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:38.450
Four mistakes to avoid.

159
00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:42.700
One: installing Hyper-V with
virtualization turned off in the firmware.

160
00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:49.350
Check systeminfo first, and turn on Intel
VT or AMD-V in the BIOS or UEFI setup.

161
00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:54.400
Two: clearing Allow management operating
system to share on the host's only

162
00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:55.850
adapter.

163
00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:59.700
The host loses its network, so keep it
checked.

164
00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:04.750
Three: choosing a Private switch when the
host must reach the VMs.

165
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.800
Private connects VMs only, so use
Internal.

166
00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:10.450
Four: leaving VM files in ProgramData.

167
00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:15.900
Set the default paths in Hyper-V Settings
before you create the first VM.

168
00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:17.750
Time to check yourself.

169
00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.400
HV01 now has Hyper-V and three switches:
External, Internal and Private.

170
00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:27.850
First, is Hyper-V a type 1 or a type 2
hypervisor, and why?

171
00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:33.700
Second, a test VM must talk to HV01 but
never to the real network.

172
00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:36.750
Which switch do you connect it to?

173
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:42.600
Third, after you create the External
switch, HV01's IP address sits on a new

174
00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:44.050
adapter.

175
00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:45.900
Which one, and why?

176
00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:50.950
And fourth, your company runs ten Windows
Server VMs on one host.

177
00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:52.400
Which edition fits?

178
00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:54.250
Pause the video now.

179
00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:55.700
One: type 1.

180
00:10:55.950 --> 00:11:01.150
The Windows hypervisor runs on the
hardware, and Windows Server runs in the

181
00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:03.800
root partition on top of it.

182
00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:05.650
Two: the Internal switch.

183
00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:09.100
It connects the host and the VMs only.

184
00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:14.150
Private would cut off the host, and
External reaches the real network.

185
00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:15.600
Three: vEthernet External.

186
00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:21.450
Sharing the adapter with the host creates
that virtual adapter, and the IP settings

187
00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:22.900
move to it.

188
00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:25.950
Four: Datacenter, with unlimited Windows
Server VMs.

189
00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:31.000
With Standard, you would have to license
all the cores five times.

190
00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:36.450
On HV01, systeminfo says a hypervisor has
been detected, because HV01 itself runs

191
00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:37.900
inside NetLab.

192
00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:43.350
I note the description of the Ethernet
adapter, then install Hyper-V in Server

193
00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:48.000
Manager, with no switch in the wizard and
